 @install_plan: ~".agents/plans/spw-site-install/PLAN.md"
 @cli_plan: ~".agents/plans/absorb-spwq-cli/PLAN.md"
}

^["interaction_depth"]{
 question_surfaces: [#open]
 decision_log: [#stream]
 taste_checkpoints: ["2026-03-27 01:08", "2026-03-27 09:10", "2026-03-27 13:26", "2026-03-27 15:19"]
 surprise_budget: "v0.3.0 shipped structurally before the release story was written — the narrative must catch up, not fabricate"
 resolution_pattern: "each release surface commit answers: what becomes truer for adopters, what does the team learn, what subject becomes easier to discuss publicly"
}

^["stream"]{
 >>[2026-03-19 23:50] observe — package.json is still v0.2.0-alpha.1 and the repo has no explicit v0.3.0 release track.
 >>[2026-03-19 23:50] observe — release scripts and waypoint surfaces exist, but March 2026 cadence is not documented inside the canon.
 >>[2026-03-19 23:50] decide — treat 2026-03-13 as a missed alpha checkpoint, recover toward a 2026-03-26 beta gate and a 2026-03-31 release gate.
 >>[2026-03-19 23:50] decide — align the release theme with the implementation plan: monorepo workspace, LSP/CLI extraction, runtime extensibility, and agent-tool decoupling.
 >>[2026-03-19 23:50] hygiene — branch is main at 7b08fda81cc24353cd8650a57299eb1effdd6bbf with no unrelated drift outside .agents/plans.
 >>[2026-03-27 01:05] observe — 2026-03-26 beta gate passed without a formal checkpoint. Package.json is now 0.3.2. Monorepo structure is live (packages/ layout, @spwashi namespace). VS Code extension has LSP integration. The release narrative needs to catch up with reality.
 >>[2026-03-27 01:06] revise — reframe: v0.3.0 has effectively shipped structurally. The remaining work is formalizing it: changelog, waypoint, npm publish readiness, VS Code Marketplace readiness, and the site-install story.
 >>[2026-03-27 01:07] decide — expand commits to cover the external-facing release gates: npm package publishability, extension marketplace metadata, CLI binary distribution, and a quick-start guide for site codebases. These are the surfaces that make v0.3.x real for external consumers.
 >>[2026-03-27 01:08] taste — the release isn't just a version bump; it's the moment external codebases can depend on @spwashi/spw-seed, @spwashi/spw-runtime, @spwashi/spw-cli, and @spwashi/spw-lsp as real packages. Every commit should move toward that being true.
 >>[2026-03-27 09:10] revise — primary distribution model changed to git submodule at .spw/_workbench, not npm packages. npm publish remains a future convenience layer but is no longer the release gate. The v0.3.0 release is real when a site codebase can git submodule add the workbench and have parser/runtime/LSP/CLI resolve from the submodule path.
 >>[2026-03-27 13:24] revise — reposition this plan as the coordination braid across install, governance, CLI taxonomy, diagnostics, and editor readiness. The release track should describe those lanes truthfully, not replace them with a packaging fantasy.
 >>[2026-03-27 13:25] decide — parallelize the remaining release work into three lanes: ecology/governance (surface readiness + waypoint), adoption story (quick-start + migration + install narrative), and discoverability (extension metadata + release verification). The release tag waits on all three.
 >>[2026-03-27 13:26] taste — ecological invigoration means external repos can engage the workbench without surrendering their identity to a package-consumer relationship. The release story should sound like that.
 >>[2026-03-27 15:18] decide — the release tag should only wait on execution-truth and public-interest lanes. Curricula and research experiments stay valuable, but they feed heuristics and discussion into release work instead of silently expanding the gate.
 >>[2026-03-27 15:19] revise — every release-adjacent commit should now answer three questions: what becomes truer for external adopters, what does the team learn, and what subject becomes easier to discuss publicly.
 >>[2026-03-28 17:49] observe — live version metadata drifted to 0.3.2 even though the active release story still treats March 31, 2026 as the truthful v0.3.0 gate. The existing local v0.3.0 tag is therefore provisional history, not the release we want to stand behind at month end.
 >>[2026-03-28 17:49] decide — reset all live workspace/package/editor version surfaces back to 0.3.0 until the release gate is actually cleared. Treat 0.3.2 as abandoned numbering preserved only in prior local history and archived planning.
 >>[2026-03-28 17:49] decide — clear the provisional local v0.3.0 tag now so March 31, 2026 can produce the real v0.3.0 tag after install, governance, discoverability, and verification agree.
 >>[2026-03-28 18:26] implement — added docs/runtime/md/quick-start.md as the first adopter-facing install surface. The guide stays on the submodule-era truth: initialize a site repo, mount .spw/_workbench, run spw:init from the mounted workbench, then verify with spw:doctor.
 >>[2026-03-28 18:26] taste — the quick-start should teach the boundary, not hide it. Commands stay slightly verbose on purpose because a truthful mount story is more valuable than pretending a site-local wrapper already exists.
 >>[2026-03-28 18:32] implement — added docs/runtime/md/migration-v02-v03.md to translate the repo from v0.2.x / lore-era assumptions into the v0.3.0 packages-era boundary: package-owned code, mounted .spw/_workbench infrastructure, explicit mount metadata, and canonical spw verbs.
 >>[2026-03-28 18:32] taste — migration notes should reward existing context without preserving old confusion. Keep compatibility aliases visible, but make the mounted boundary and package-era naming feel like the new center of gravity.
 >>[2026-03-28 18:49] implement — added docs/runtime/md/site-install-release-story.md to explain why submodule engagement at .spw/_workbench is the current public release shape: visible repo relationship, explicit mount contract, site-owned canon, and reversible infrastructure rather than a prematurely cleaner npm-first promise.
 >>[2026-03-28 18:49] taste — this note should defend the boundary rather than romanticize it. The best release copy makes the submodule model feel intentional, truthful, and limited to what the repo can already support.
 >>[2026-03-28 18:56] implement — updated .spw/surfaces/domains.spw so the registry names the installable gate explicitly and gives current active surfaces readiness cohorts with audience, phase, interaction mode, QA gate, and team shape instead of leaving release-facing metadata implicit.
 >>[2026-03-28 18:56] taste — readiness metadata should compress launch judgment, not bureaucratize it. The registry only gets better if it helps release copy, install truth, and governance say the same thing with fewer hidden assumptions.
 >>[2026-03-28 19:05] implement — updated extensions/vscode-spw/package.json and README.md so marketplace-facing metadata now names the extension as a preview thin client, adds repository/homepage/bugs/keywords/license fields, and makes the current workbench-checkout startup boundary explicit instead of implying mounted-site readiness that the extension does not yet deliver.
 >>[2026-03-28 19:05] taste — discoverability metadata should narrow claims before it expands them. The right preview badge and README sentence are better than a broader marketplace promise that the current startup path cannot yet defend.
}

